From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] touch: calibration works when the stored touch range is
 damaged

The stored touch range (settings.json /input/touch_range) is programmed
into lv_evdev at start, and a calibration capture only turned the affine
off: the stored range stayed live, and lv_evdev scales and clamps every
reading through it. With a damaged range (issue #90: X 707..19420,
Y -947..-26692 on a 0..4095 device) every tap clamped to Y=0, the panel
rejected the three points as degenerate before the fit from the raw
readings was reached, and no recalibration could ever finish, the hold
to recalibrate (0089-0091) included.

- The capture runs under the device's declared ABS range in place of a
  stored one (TouchCalibrationSession::begin_capture, through the new
  ICalibrationSink::declared_touch_range), laid out as the stored range
  and the affine stored with it run together (range_layout_with_affine,
  orient_declared_like): the stored swap, and each axis reversed when
  the range reverses it XOR the affine's term for it (a, e) is
  negative; an affine whose off-diagonal terms dominate swaps the axes
  once more, with the signs of b and d. Calibrations made on Kiln's
  kernels keep the Y flip in the range, ones converted from stock units
  in the affine (e < 0). Either way the screen stays the right way up
  during the capture and the overlay's Cancel and the wizard's Skip are
  where they are drawn. Without a stored range (none, or one refused below) the
  declared range is taken the way the device runs uncalibrated
  (uncalibrated_declared_orientation): Y reversed on Kiln builds on
  Kiln's own kernels (kiln_raw_y_runs_up: the TSC2007's raw readings,
  where Flashforge's 5.10 driver flips Y), as the seed's range does;
  elsewhere as declared, and then nothing is replaced. An environment
  override and a display-size range stay.
- The snapshot keeps the range live at the start: cancel puts it back
  (restore), a retry or fast-revert runs under the capture range again
  (revert_for_retry), and an accepted calibration saves its fitted range
  as before. An affine-only result is saved with the range it was
  captured under (commit_calibration_result's new capture_range), so the
  next start runs the range its affine was solved over.
- Every successful commit clears /input/force_calibration, so a forced
  calibration taken in the wizard's touch step is not forced again by
  the overlay at the next start.
- The Settings overlay's "no usable new calibration" path puts the whole
  pre-session mapping back through the session (show_pre_session), so
  the stored range comes back with its affine.
- Capture markers map the digitizer reading through the pre-session
  range (evdev_point_from_raw), so they still land where the touch went
  under the old calibration.
- load_touch_range() refuses a stored range through which the device's
  declared range reaches under 1/8 of a screen axis
  (stored_touch_range_fits_declared, kStoredTouchRangeMinReach): that
  axis is in effect clamped to one edge. Either direction is fine (min >
  max inverts an axis); the swap is taken into account. It logs a
  warning and the kernel's range applies; load_touch_calibration() drops
  the affine stored with such a range, and the refusal sets
  /input/force_calibration (saved), so the calibration opens at this
  start, through the capture above, as when ad5x-touch-units sets the
  flag. Both backends set the declared range first
  (set_declared_touch_range, trusted_declared_touch_range: not when the
  query failed, an axis has no span, or the range is the display
  transposed). With no declared range known nothing is judged, as
  upstream.

Why a reach and not a margin around the declared range: a panel that
emits past its declared range saves a wider range with its own
calibration, and a margin refused that at the next start, every start.
Its readings reach 1/k of the axis at k times the declared span, so only
k over 8 is refused, while the damaged record's Y reaches none of it.

Tests in test_kiln_touch_cal_range.cpp: the reported range clamps every
tap to Y=0 and the panel never reaches VERIFY; under the session the
capture runs under the kernel range the stored one's way up, the fit
finds the panel's edges and the commit saves them; with a good, Y-up
stored range, and with the owner's state from before issue #90 (X
307..3702, Y 649..3482, e = -1), the bottom-left and bottom-right
buttons map to the bottom during the capture; a flip in the range and
one in the affine cancel out; the layout of a range and an affine,
off-diagonal included; and the stored range comes back on cancel, after a
retry and with the pre-session mapping shown; the swap and directions
carried over; nothing is replaced under an environment or display-size
range, with no declared range, or where the declared range is live the
same way up; a forced calibration with no stored range runs Y-up on
Kiln's kernels, finishes, saves the fitted range and clears the flag; an
affine-only result saves the capture range; every successful commit
clears the flag; load_touch_range refuses the reported range and sets
the flag, and accepts normal and inverted ones (Y 3608..591, X
4000..90) and the wide range a panel emitting past its range saves; the
affine stored with a refused range is not loaded; the helpers.

Upstream: not applicable (Kiln OS specific)
